  "main_text": "<think>\nOkay, let's see. The user is Isabella Johansson with zip code 32286. She wants to find her order that was sent to Texas by accident. She needs the tracking number and return all items except the pet bed. She doesn't remember the order number.\n\nFirst, I already used the find_user_id_by_name_zip tool and got her user ID: isabella_johansson_2152. Now, I need to get her user details to find the orders. So the next step is to call get_user_details with her user ID. That will show her orders, addresses, etc. Once I have the orders, I can look for the one sent to Texas. Then, check the order status. If it's delivered, she can return items. But she wants to return all except the pet bed, so I need to get the item IDs of the order. Also, she needs the tracking number, which is in the order details. \n\nWait, but the user might have multiple orders. I need to list all her orders and find the one with the Texas address. Once found, check the status. If it's delivered, proceed with return. But the return function requires item IDs. So after getting the order details, I can extract the tracking ID and the item IDs. Then, use return_delivered_order_items with the order ID, item IDs (excluding the pet bed), and payment method. But I need to confirm which payment method she wants for the refund. Also, check if the order is eligible for return (status delivered). \n\nBut first, let's get the user details to find the orders. So the next tool call should be get_user_details with her user ID. Then, process the response to find the relevant order.\n</think>\n\n<tool_call>\n{\"name\": \"get_user_details\", \"arguments\": {\"user_id\": \"isabella_johansson_2152\"}}\n",
